WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:03.710
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Hi.

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:04.585
I'm Maya Kaczorowski.

00:00:04.585 --> 00:00:07.130
And I'm here at KubeCon
EU walking the floor.

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:09.106
And I'm here with
Kamil from GitLab.

00:00:09.106 --> 00:00:10.710
Kamil, how are you doing today?

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.210
KAMIL TRZCINSKI:
Oh, I'm quite busy.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.992
Actually we-- first,
we actually are

00:00:13.992 --> 00:00:15.200
talking with a lot of people.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.140
They are very interested in
our Kubernetes integration

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.770
that we started offering.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.920
And there is also a
lot of work and a lot

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.260
of very nice ideas
about the feature

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.940
that we plan to introduce.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.610
So basically, we are
able to meet with people

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:32.532
to discuss our plans and
what they actually each are.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:34.740
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So tell
me a bit more about GitLab.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.910
What are some of the things
that GitLab currently does?

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.190
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: We
actually spent a lot of time

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.190
on trying to be
the best on devops.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:46.460
We introduced Auto
DevOps, which kind of

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:51.770
follows the strength of
being very good integrated,

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.950
integrated with the
Kubernetes basically,

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:58.140
simplifying interaction
with the Kubernetes.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.820
Because Kubernetes by
itself, it's a great product.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:05.690
But it's also quite complex
to people starting using that.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.490
We have this kube-apiserver.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.640
We have this
deployment manifest.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.000
And then we have the Helm.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.300
These are not easy
things to teach.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.780
Their learning
curve is quite high.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.840
But with Auto DevOps
we simplify all of that

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:25.385
into a set of conventions
and our implementation

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:26.260
of these conventions.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.730
So you can start
right like that.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:30.755
You just push your
application to GitLab.

00:01:30.755 --> 00:01:32.420
We test your application.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.370
We perform security scanning.

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:37.400
We create a container in
each of your applications

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.030
and post a container registry,
then provide your staging

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.290
and production environments.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:48.110
And actually when you even go
to our Kubernetes integration,

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:51.320
you just click a button, log
into Google, to your Google

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.880
Cloud, and maybe attach
your credit card,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.160
and in five minutes you have
working Kubernetes cluster

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.190
and your working
application, maybe

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.830
the application that
is based on the beatbox

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.164
that our hierarchy is providing.

00:02:05.164 --> 00:02:07.413
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So I can
deploy directly from GitLab

00:02:07.413 --> 00:02:08.570
to Kubernetes engine?

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:09.259
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: Exactly. right.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:09.810
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
That's amazing.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.185
KAMIL TRZCINSKI:
It's like-- it's

00:02:11.185 --> 00:02:13.460
even more like
directly from GitLab

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.560
using Google Cloud
to Kubernetes engine.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.470
So we try to remove
all the barriers

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.889
on deploying your application.

00:02:20.889 --> 00:02:22.430
You just care about
your application.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.500
You don't care about
the infrastructure.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.689
You don't care about
the containers.

00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:27.980
You care about the application.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.180
You care about the workflow.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.730
You care about the
practices, something

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.590
that I know that
this is probably

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:37.250
one of the most requested
questions that we have now.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.000
What is the best practice
to work with the Kubernetes?

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.000
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Right.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.170
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: There
is so much technology

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:43.440
right now in the market.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.880
And so many different ideas
that people are basically lost.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.570
And we try to solve that need.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:49.810
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Try help
people out, help developers.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:50.120
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: Yes.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.710
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: That's great.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:52.460
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: Thank you.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:53.450
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
And anything else you

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.658
want to talk about here,
while we're here at KubeCon?

00:02:58.057 --> 00:03:00.140
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: We have
very big plans for the CI

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.415
integration on GitLab.

00:03:01.415 --> 00:03:03.000
There are a lot of
things happening.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.040
For example, I spent time
with our contributors,

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.200
right before this conversation,
about the next upcoming feature

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.070
for the CI, which is like
ability to debug jobs live,

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.170
using the web
terminal, something

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:23.390
that is becoming a
fundament to Web IDE

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.670
that we are right now
working on that got

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:28.580
introduced, I think
in 10.7 or it's

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:29.872
going to be introduced in 10.8.

00:03:29.872 --> 00:03:30.746
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: OK.

00:03:30.746 --> 00:03:32.960
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: And Web
IDE is actually quite cool.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:33.720
Because--

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.300
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
What does it do?

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.595
KAMIL TRZCINSKI:
Consider that you start--

00:03:37.595 --> 00:03:40.060
usually we have this tendency,
like we open our terminal.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.850
We our open our VSCode or some
other IDE on our computers.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.080
And we start writing.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.020
We commit, push, maybe
we run some tests.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.835
But let's say that
you don't need that.

00:03:49.835 --> 00:03:52.070
Let's say that you have
your computer, which

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.892
is a terminal, like a terminal
to the world, to the Cloud.

00:03:54.892 --> 00:03:57.350
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Right, you
just want to use that directly

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:57.975
for a computer.

00:03:57.975 --> 00:03:59.960
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: And you
just open the browser.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:01.625
We just provision
you everything.

00:04:01.625 --> 00:04:03.000
We just give you
the source code.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.330
We provision you an
environment workspace

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:06.920
where you can work
on much beefier

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.079
machines than your
lightweight MacBook, right?

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:11.495
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Yes, understood.

00:04:11.495 --> 00:04:12.770
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: So It makes--

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:16.130
it's actually way
easier, faster,

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:19.819
because it's much easier to
switch between different work

00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:21.649
spaces, so between
different tasks,

00:04:21.649 --> 00:04:27.110
and remove you from trying to do
all these heavy-duty operations

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.840
on your computer, which
are [INAUDIBLE] kind

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:31.130
of under-powered.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:35.560
Like GitHub itself, we
are running 27,000 tests

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:36.320
every commit.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:37.820
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Wow, that's a lot.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.655
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: So
it's crazy when you

00:04:39.655 --> 00:04:41.210
will try to run that locally.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.140
So we basically try
to use that power up

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:47.540
of the GitHub, and
GitHub CI, and Web IDE,

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:50.330
and the terminal integration
to move everything

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:54.110
into Cloud, where you can have
basically unlimited resources.

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:57.620
And if you consider that you
can use that with the Kubernetes

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.610
and Google Cloud,
something that we offer,

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:03.010
it makes it basically an
infinite scalable solution

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.499
that you are never
limited by anything.

00:05:05.499 --> 00:05:07.290
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Thank
you so much, Kamil.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:08.550
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: Thank you.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:09.720
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: I hope
you're having a good KubeCon

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:10.410
right now.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:11.320
KAMIL TRZCINSKI: It's amazing.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:12.180
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Good.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.230
And to all those
watching, I hope

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.140
you're enjoying watching
these videos of us at KubeCon.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.181
We'll see you in Seattle.

